2026-10-25 — STAT 566 handwritten-notes conversion

Scope: TASK.md; notes/stat566-note01.pdf (9 pp), stat566-note02.pdf (22 pp),
stat566-note3.pdf (12 pp), stat566-note5.pdf (10 pp). Total 53 source pages.
No source Note 4 was supplied. Original filenames/numbering retained.

Input PDFs contain a noisy recognition text layer. All source pages were also
rendered and visually inspected; mathematical content was edited using the images
and local lecture PDFs, not blindly copied from OCR. Chinese annotations translated
to English. Repeated material consolidated; diagrams rendered as vector diagrams
or explicit graph edge/path lists. SOURCE-MAP.md accounts for every source page.

Outputs: tex-notes/{stat566-note01,stat566-note02,stat566-note3,stat566-note5}.tex
and matching PDFs; stat566-all.tex/PDF; shared preamble and chapter sources;
build.py, README.md, SOURCE-MAP.md, LOG.log. The full source tree is reproducible.

Major corrections:
- Outcome response types versus IV treatment compliance types.
- Consistency is Y=Y(A), not independence from intention; separate exchangeability.
- Neyman finite-population versus within-arm variances; conservative in expectation;
  centered asymptotic statistic, not an automatically exact normal statistic.
- MTR upper bound retained at the data-dependent Manski upper endpoint.
- MTS alone does not give lower bound zero; MTR+MTS does.
- Fork/collider arrow labels interchanged in handwritten note02 p. 5.
- Four-node graph implies Y independent of A given Z,M, not given M alone.
- Mutual independent SCM noise implies Markov, not automatically faithfulness.
- Markov equivalence, observational versus causal semantics, and intervention
  uniqueness claims qualified. Back-door identification does not need faithfulness.
- Pooled t-test denominator includes arm-size factors.
- Truncated factorization evaluated at intervention; no stray indicator in marginal Y law.
- Baseline adjustment can cause M-bias with latent causes; valid sets need path checks.
- Wright causal path formula separated from covariance/error variance formula.
- IPW supports continuous outcomes; logistic propensity response is treatment A.
- Symmetric score clipping; clipping versus population-changing trimming.
- ATT averages only over treated observations; distinguish baseline CATE.
- Conditional IV includes controls in both stages; IV SEs need proper inference.
- IV exclusion versus exogeneity; first-stage heuristics and DWH non-rejection qualified.
- Linear homogeneous IV effect versus binary-instrument LATE.

TODO handling / limitations:
- Yellow boxes preserve all explicit TODO marks, including reread consistency,
  Fisher, timestamps 10:08 and 9:02, MTR bounds, SCM, IPW intuition/proof,
  H ajek intuition, and standardized mean differences.
- Timestamp-only missing recording segments cannot be reconstructed exactly.
  Core lecture content is supplied and labeled, without pretending to recover audio.
- The abbreviation EQC? on note01 p. 3 is not confidently deciphered; preserved.
- DID for IV — Ting Ye on note5 p. 6 remains an attributed reading prompt.
- Note3 pp. 11–12 and note5 pp. 8–9 have headings/blank space only; additions are
  labeled lecture supplements. No unprovided handwriting was invented.
- TASK.md requests $$ for inline math, although standard TeX uses it for displays.
  The preamble defines active paired $$ delimiters as ensuremath, permitting the
  requested source syntax and true inline layout. Displays use equation/align.
- The template's two font packages were replaced by one coherent New Computer
  Modern text/math setup. Margins, 12pt size, and remark-box style retained.
- PDF skill marker initially had a stale plugin-version path after a live plugin
  update. Located the installed marker and successfully ran it once.
- Initial sandbox compilation failed because LuaLaTeX could not write its font cache;
  reran compilation through the required permission mechanism.

